hi if you support the rule of law  sometimes it's the small victories you  have to celebrate that's what we got  this week in a czar vs. alina health  services decided by the Supreme Court in  that case we found that often Congress  is supposed to make the laws of the  country that's what our Constitution  says but in reality almost all of our  laws are actually made by bureaucrats  while that's a violation of our  constitutional separation of powers we  do have a system to try to limit what  agencies do on their own and that limit  is to force them to give notice to the  public ahead of time of what they are  going to regulate and allow comment by  the public of what they believe should  be done about a particular rule now the  government tried to violate this even  the small limit in the past case that  was discussed this week what the  government tried to do was to skirt this  even small limit on the bureaucracies  power the Medicare system gives  reimbursements to low income for  low-income patients when they go to  hospitals and the government tried to  change the rules on how they give these  reimbursements to the hospital systems  now what the government tried to argue  here and then the court rejected  seven-to-one was that they didn't have  to follow these rules and what the court  had said in that 71 ruling isn't of  course enough to restore constitutional  government it's not a full restoration  of the separation of powers but it is a  small victory for the rule of law in the  following ways it does require that a  law exists before it can be enforced it  does require that a law be followed as  written by Congress it does require that  the public know about the law before  they can be punished or have their lives  changed regarding it and finally it  gives a tiny bit of power and leeway to  the public to give some consent of the  governed to the rule making our  bureaucrats do is that enough to restore  a constitutional government  is it enough to restore the rule of law  no but it's a small step in the right  direction
 

